1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,180
Imagine a small drone
flying low over a field.

2
00:00:03,608 --> 00:00:05,348
It looks like any other drone.

3
00:00:05,588 --> 00:00:08,388
But behind it, something is unwinding.

4
00:00:08,761 --> 00:00:11,981
A thread about as thick as the
thinnest string on a guitar.

5
00:00:12,439 --> 00:00:16,819
And that thread can be twenty
kilometres long. Some are now forty.

6
00:00:17,793 --> 00:00:20,753
The drone isn't talking
to its pilot by radio.

7
00:00:21,413 --> 00:00:23,513
It's connected by a strand of glass.

8
00:00:24,491 --> 00:00:27,171
In the age of satellites
and wireless everything,

9
00:00:27,251 --> 00:00:29,671
why would anyone fly a drone on a wire?

10
00:00:31,731 --> 00:00:33,751
The answer starts with a number.

11
00:00:34,051 --> 00:00:38,111
In 2023, a British defence
think tank estimated that

12
00:00:38,341 --> 00:00:41,411
Ukraine was losing around
ten thousand drones a month.

13
00:00:41,719 --> 00:00:45,819
And a big part of the reason
wasn't guns. It was jamming.

14
00:00:47,047 --> 00:00:49,487
A radio drone runs on two signals.

15
00:00:49,607 --> 00:00:52,167
Video goes back to the pilot.

16
00:00:52,327 --> 00:00:54,707
Commands come forward to the drone.

17
00:00:55,103 --> 00:00:57,703
And a jammer doesn't need
to hack either of them.

18
00:00:58,263 --> 00:01:00,883
It just shouts louder
on the same frequencies.

19
00:01:01,535 --> 00:01:03,855
The drone can no longer hear its pilot.

20
00:01:04,655 --> 00:01:07,735
It drifts, or it lands, or
it falls out of the sky.

21
00:01:08,681 --> 00:01:14,561
Designers moved to new frequencies. The
jammers followed. Then they moved again.

22
00:01:15,424 --> 00:01:17,984
So someone asked a much simpler question.

23
00:01:18,704 --> 00:01:21,204
What if the drone didn't
use the air at all?

24
00:01:22,714 --> 00:01:25,454
An optical fibre carries light, not radio.

25
00:01:25,974 --> 00:01:29,634
Pulses of light, bouncing
along inside a strand of glass.

26
00:01:30,385 --> 00:01:32,845
Commands go out through the fibre.

27
00:01:33,065 --> 00:01:35,025
Video comes back through the same fibre.

28
00:01:36,094 --> 00:01:38,334
A jammer can fill the sky with noise.

29
00:01:38,754 --> 00:01:41,534
But this drone isn't listening to the sky.

30
00:01:43,784 --> 00:01:46,264
There's just one part
that sounds impossible.

31
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:51,400
Twenty kilometres of cable, dragged behind
a drone that weighs a few kilograms?

32
00:01:52,495 --> 00:01:53,835
It isn't dragged.

33
00:01:54,402 --> 00:01:58,822
The spool rides on the drone, and the
fibre unwinds from it as it flies.

34
00:01:59,782 --> 00:02:02,882
Whatever is already laid out
just lies still on the ground.

35
00:02:03,409 --> 00:02:05,529
The drone never pulls its tail.

36
00:02:06,149 --> 00:02:09,489
It only lays it down, the
way a spider leaves a thread.

37
00:02:10,121 --> 00:02:13,661
That's how something this fragile
survives the flight at all.

38
00:02:14,227 --> 00:02:17,707
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

39
00:02:18,327 --> 00:02:19,947
It really helps the channel.

40
00:02:22,317 --> 00:02:24,157
And the idea isn't new.

41
00:02:24,545 --> 00:02:26,545
For more than half a century, anti-tank

42
00:02:26,625 --> 00:02:29,705
missiles have unspooled
a thin wire behind them.

43
00:02:30,025 --> 00:02:33,625
One of the best known, TOW,
literally ends in "wire-guided".

44
00:02:35,011 --> 00:02:38,151
In the 1980s, the US
Army replaced that wire

45
00:02:38,381 --> 00:02:40,771
with fibre and put a camera in the nose.

46
00:02:41,207 --> 00:02:44,627
The missile sent its picture back
down the fibre, and the operator

47
00:02:44,857 --> 00:02:48,987
could steer it from behind a hill,
seeing the target only on a screen.

48
00:02:49,845 --> 00:02:53,605
It was cancelled. Then
revived. Then cancelled again.

49
00:02:54,105 --> 00:02:55,845
Mostly, it was too expensive.

50
00:02:57,535 --> 00:03:00,275
What changed is the
price of everything else.

51
00:03:00,637 --> 00:03:03,357
The telecom industry now makes hundreds of

52
00:03:03,587 --> 00:03:06,857
millions of kilometres of
optical fibre every year.

53
00:03:06,989 --> 00:03:10,189
And a small racing drone
costs a few hundred dollars.

54
00:03:10,540 --> 00:03:13,880
Put the two together, and an
idea that was too expensive for a

55
00:03:14,110 --> 00:03:18,080
missile programme becomes cheap
enough to throw away after one flight.

56
00:03:19,185 --> 00:03:24,405
By 2025, both sides of the war were
using fibre drones in large numbers.

57
00:03:27,075 --> 00:03:30,855
And it turned out that beating the
jammer was only the first advantage.

58
00:03:31,609 --> 00:03:33,989
A radio drone isn't just listening.

59
00:03:34,349 --> 00:03:36,009
It's also transmitting.

60
00:03:36,189 --> 00:03:37,949
And so is its pilot.

61
00:03:38,534 --> 00:03:42,634
Every signal can be picked up, and
traced back to where it came from.

62
00:03:43,110 --> 00:03:45,130
A pilot on a fibre is silent.

63
00:03:45,850 --> 00:03:47,750
There's nothing in the air to trace.

64
00:03:48,435 --> 00:03:53,135
And radio struggles when there's
something in the way. A hill. A forest.

65
00:03:53,215 --> 00:03:54,475
A concrete wall.

66
00:03:54,825 --> 00:03:57,145
Light inside a fibre doesn't care.

67
00:03:57,665 --> 00:04:01,005
Wherever the drone can go,
the signal goes with it.

68
00:04:02,275 --> 00:04:06,295
Unjammable. Silent. Works behind
hills and inside buildings.

69
00:04:08,675 --> 00:04:10,855
So why isn't every drone on a wire?

70
00:04:13,685 --> 00:04:15,125
Start with the weight.

71
00:04:15,462 --> 00:04:18,342
The fibre carries
information, not electricity.

72
00:04:18,842 --> 00:04:21,562
The drone still runs on its own battery.

73
00:04:22,023 --> 00:04:24,963
And the spool has to come
along for the whole flight.

74
00:04:25,703 --> 00:04:28,483
A ten-kilometre spool
weighs about a kilogram.

75
00:04:29,054 --> 00:04:31,954
That's weight the drone
can't carry as anything else.

76
00:04:32,594 --> 00:04:35,974
They're described as sluggish,
and easily pushed around by wind.

77
00:04:37,714 --> 00:04:38,974
Then there's the length.

78
00:04:39,750 --> 00:04:43,650
When the spool is empty, the drone
has reached the end of its leash.

79
00:04:44,047 --> 00:04:46,407
And it's not a leash you can wind back in.

80
00:04:46,827 --> 00:04:48,667
Each fibre is laid once.

81
00:04:50,337 --> 00:04:51,917
And then there's the tail.

82
00:04:52,395 --> 00:04:56,335
Imagine flying through a forest
while laying a thread behind you.

83
00:04:56,479 --> 00:04:58,139
It can catch on branches.

84
00:04:58,759 --> 00:05:00,919
It can snag on wires and buildings.

85
00:05:01,761 --> 00:05:03,841
So the pilot has two questions, not one.

86
00:05:04,071 --> 00:05:05,961
Can the drone get there?

87
00:05:06,191 --> 00:05:07,721
And can the fibre?

88
00:05:09,309 --> 00:05:12,169
But the strangest problem
is that the thread points.

89
00:05:12,625 --> 00:05:16,645
It lies on the ground, from the
drone all the way back to the pilot.

90
00:05:17,135 --> 00:05:21,215
Where it hangs in grass and
branches, it glints in low sunlight.

91
00:05:21,955 --> 00:05:25,355
And from the air, a line like that
can be followed back to the pilot.

92
00:05:26,572 --> 00:05:28,592
So the defence changed too.

93
00:05:28,992 --> 00:05:32,112
If you can't jam a drone, you
have to stop it physically.

94
00:05:32,885 --> 00:05:35,605
Along some supply roads,
both sides now stretch

95
00:05:35,835 --> 00:05:38,405
nets over the road itself, for kilometres.

96
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:42,520
A tunnel of netting, because the
airwaves no longer protect you.

97
00:05:44,520 --> 00:05:46,020
That's the pattern here.

98
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:49,580
The fibre didn't solve the
drone problem. It moved it.

99
00:05:49,943 --> 00:05:52,143
An invisible link that
could be jammed became a

100
00:05:52,373 --> 00:05:55,123
physical one that can be
tangled, cut and followed.

101
00:05:57,633 --> 00:06:01,993
And there's one more place the
problem moved to. The ground itself.

102
00:06:03,030 --> 00:06:06,910
One flight leaves kilometres of
fibre lying across the landscape.

103
00:06:07,349 --> 00:06:11,009
Now multiply that by every
fibre drone flown on this front.

104
00:06:11,447 --> 00:06:14,087
In some places, fields and
tree lines shimmer with

105
00:06:14,317 --> 00:06:17,627
threads, like a spider's
web the size of a valley.

106
00:06:18,378 --> 00:06:21,878
Glass, in a plastic
coating. It doesn't rot.

107
00:06:22,458 --> 00:06:24,898
At most, it crumbles into microplastic.

108
00:06:27,018 --> 00:06:32,298
And in June 2026, a photo from eastern
Ukraine spread around the world.

109
00:06:32,595 --> 00:06:36,855
A bird's nest, knocked out of a tree
by a bomb. Woven out of grass...

110
00:06:36,935 --> 00:06:38,135
and drone fibre.

111
00:06:38,901 --> 00:06:43,441
That same month, the army sent two
nests like it to a war museum in Kyiv.

112
00:06:44,125 --> 00:06:47,445
Nobody knows yet how much
harm the fibre does to birds.

113
00:06:47,685 --> 00:06:51,325
But long thin threads can
wrap around legs and wings.

114
00:06:51,965 --> 00:06:54,445
Owls have already been
found hanging in them.

115
00:06:56,865 --> 00:06:59,045
For a century, we've been cutting wires.

116
00:06:59,885 --> 00:07:03,005
Phones, networks, aircraft.
Wireless everything.

117
00:07:03,377 --> 00:07:06,117
And in the most crowded
radio sky on Earth,

118
00:07:06,197 --> 00:07:08,517
the answer turned out to be a wire again.

119
00:07:10,347 --> 00:07:13,127
When the war ends, the
jammers will be switched off,

120
00:07:13,267 --> 00:07:15,967
and the noise in the air
will stop that same day.

121
00:07:17,123 --> 00:07:18,713
The threads will still be there.
